.TH MAN 6
.SH NAME
man \- macros to typeset manual
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mandoc -man
.I file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
These macros are used to lay out the reference pages in this manual.
They are the
.B -man
macros of
.IR troff ,
in the dialect Plan 9's manual uses,
restricted to what
.I mandoc
on the host renders.
A page is a plain text file under
.BI man/ section / name\fR,
with no suffix.
The section directories are:
.TP 6
.B 1
commands a user types at
.IR rc (1)
.TP
.B 2
system calls, and the libraries under
.B sys/
.TP
.B 3
kernel devices, reached as
.BI # letter
and bound into a namespace
.TP
.B 4
file servers that run as programs and post a name in
.B /srv
.TP
.B 5
the file protocol, 9P2000.L, one message per page
.TP
.B 6
file formats and conventions
.TP
.B 8
booting, building, and running the machine
.TP
.B 9
the kernel, for those who change it
.PD
.SS Layout of a page
The first line is
.BI ".TH " NAME " " section\fR,
with the name in capitals and the section a single digit.
Then the sections, in this order and with these names,
omitting any that do not apply:
.IP
.EX
\&.SH NAME
\&.SH SYNOPSIS
\&.SH DESCRIPTION
\&.SH EXAMPLES
\&.SH FILES
\&.SH SOURCE
\&.SH SEE ALSO
\&.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\&.SH BUGS
.EE
.PP
.B NAME
is one line:
the names the page documents, separated by commas, then
.BR \e- ,
then a short lower-case phrase.
The index of each section is made from these lines.
.PP
.B SYNOPSIS
gives the command line, the procedure signatures, or the file names.
A command's options are listed in square brackets, with
.B .B
for the literal parts and
.B .I
for the parts a user supplies.
A library page lists the
.B import
line and then one signature per line inside
.BR ".nf" / ".fi" ,
each in
.BR .B .
A device page lists the
.B bind
that makes it visible and the files it serves.
.PP
.B DESCRIPTION
says what the thing does, in the present tense, in declarative sentences.
It describes what exists, not what was planned, not what was tried,
and not the order things were built in.
Reasons are given where a reader needs them to use the thing correctly,
in a sentence or two, and not otherwise.
.PP
.B EXAMPLES
shows a use, inside
.BR ".IP" / ".EX" / ".EE" .
.B FILES
names the files a program reads or serves.
.B SOURCE
names the directory or file in the tree, relative to its root,
in
.BR .B .
.B SEE ALSO
lists related pages as
.BI ".IR " name " (" section ")" ,
separated by commas,
and never names a document outside the manual.
.B DIAGNOSTICS
says what the exit status is, or what an error looks like.
.B BUGS
records what is known to be missing or wrong,
plainly, in a sentence each.
.SS Fonts
.TP
.B .I
the name of a command, a system call or a variable in running text,
and a word that stands for something the user supplies
.TP
.B .B
a file name, a flag, a literal, a keyword,
and anything typed exactly as shown
.TP
.B .IR
alternating italic and roman, for a cross reference:
.B ".IR rc (1)"
.TP
.B .BR
alternating bold and roman, for a literal followed by punctuation:
.B ".BR /dev/cons ,"
.TP
.B .BI
alternating bold and italic, for a file name with a variable part:
.B ".BI /proc/ n /status"
.TP
.B .RI
alternating roman and italic
.PD
.PP
Punctuation that follows a font change goes on the same line as the
last word, after a space, so it is set in roman and closed up:
.B ".IR bind (1),"
rather than a comma on a line of its own.
A word is never split across two macro lines.
The minus sign in a flag is written
.BR \e- .
A dash in prose is two hyphens.
.SS Blocks
.TP
.B .PP
a new paragraph
.TP
.B .SS
a subsection heading
.TP
.BI ".TP " width
a tagged paragraph: the next line is the tag, the lines after it the body.
The first
.B .TP
of a list may give a width in ens.
A list of tags ends with
.B .PD
when it was started with
.BR ".TP " width\fR,
so the spacing goes back to normal.
.TP
.B ".IP \e(bu"
a bulleted paragraph
.TP
.BR ".EX" / ".EE"
literal text, in a fixed-width font, indented by a preceding
.B .IP
.TP
.BR ".nf" / ".fi"
lines kept as written, in the current font
.TP
.B .br
a line break
.PD
.PP
The Plan 9 macros
.BR .L ,
.BR .LR ,
.B .TF
and
.B .RL
are not used, because the host's
.I mandoc
does not know them.
.SS Prose
Sentences are short and have one idea each.
The subject is the thing described, and the verb is active.
There are no semicolons, no parentheses around whole sentences,
and no exclamation.
A page addresses nobody:
it says what a command does, not what you can do with it.
A term is defined where it is first used, and then used consistently.
Numbers that a reader has to know are stated exactly.
Where Vectra departs from Plan 9 in something a Plan 9 reader would assume,
the page says so in one sentence.
.SS Checking
.IP
.EX
make -C man check
.EE
.PP
renders every page with
.I mandoc
and reports what does not parse,
then checks that every cross reference names a page that exists
and that the sections of each page are in order.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B man/lib/checkman.awk
the cross-reference and ordering check
.TP
.BI man/ n /INDEX
one line per name, made by
.B make index
.SH SOURCE
.B man/Makefile
.br
.B man/lib
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (1)
